thena spine> :theorem idp {l} : ∀ (A : Type {l}) -> A -> A
proving idp : ∀ (A : Type (ℓ1)) -> A -> A
thena spine> try (\ (A : Type {l}) (a : A) -> a)
thena spine> solve
thena spine> qed
idp : ∀ (A : Type (ℓ1)) -> A -> A   ∎
thena spine> :infer idp {0}
idp : ∀ (A : Type₀) -> A -> A
thena spine> :infer idp {1}
idp : ∀ (A : Type₁) -> A -> A
thena spine> :infer idp {2}
idp : ∀ (A : Type₂) -> A -> A
thena spine> :infer idp
idp has 1 level parameter, and was given 0 level arguments
its level parameters are prenex, so a use supplies all of them or none
thena spine> :infer idp {0 1}
idp has 1 level parameter, and was given 2 level arguments
its level parameters are prenex, so a use supplies all of them or none
thena spine> :core Type {l}
l is not a level parameter in scope
thena spine> :theorem t2 : Type₀
proving t2 : Type₀
thena spine> assume x {l} : Type₀
only a definition has level parameters; this binds a component
thena spine> :quit
